  "theory": {
"intro": "A binomial numerator such as $x+3$ acts as a single quantity, so keep it in brackets. Rewrite both fractions over a common denominator, then expand and collect like terms.",
"facts": [
          "To make an equivalent fraction, multiply **every term** of the numerator by the same factor as the denominator: $\\frac{4x+7}{3} = \\frac{5(4x+7)}{15} = \\frac{20x+35}{15}$.",
          "Dividing works the same way: $\\frac{3x+9}{18} = \\frac{x+3}{6}$, with every term divided by $3$.",
          "Once the denominators match, combine the numerators over the one denominator: $\\frac{a}{n} \\pm \\frac{b}{n} = \\frac{a \\pm b}{n}$.",
          "Bracket a binomial numerator before subtracting, because the minus sign multiplies **every** term: $\\frac{x-1}{3} - \\frac{x+4}{5} = \\frac{5(x-1) - 3(x+4)}{15}$.",
          "Expanding a negative across a bracket changes the sign of each term: $-3(x+4) = -3x-12$ and $-6(3-x) = -18+6x$.",
          "Finish by collecting like terms in the numerator, then check whether the whole fraction has a common factor: $\\frac{4x+8}{6} = \\frac{2x+4}{3}$.",
          "A negative sign can sit with the fraction or with the numerator: $-\\frac{a+b}{c} = \\frac{-a-b}{c}$."
        ],
"steps": [
          "Rewrite the fractions with the lowest common denominator.",
          "Add or subtract the numerators.",
          "Expand and simplify."
        ],
